Touching grass is a good start. But I think there’s something much deeper here.

To regain touch with reality, you need experiences that aren’t entirely controlled by your thoughts, expectations, narratives, or the systems you’re immersed in.

You need reality to have a chance to surprise you, challenge you, reward you, and remind you what actually matters.

And there are different kinds of grounding.

Six ways to reconnect with reality

1. Physical grounding

Walk outdoors, feel sunlight, exercise, swim, work with your hands. Engage your senses without constantly narrating the experience. Get back into your body rather than only observing life through thoughts.

2. Relational grounding

Play with your children, laugh with your wife, spend time with friends. Other people have their own minds and experiences. They remind you that reality isn’t limited to your internal story.

3. Consequential grounding

Build something, repair something, cook, garden, or learn a physical skill. The world gives you independent feedback. The board fits or it doesn’t. The plant grows or it doesn’t.

4. Novelty and adventure

Explore unfamiliar places, learn new things, meet different people. New experiences interrupt habitual predictions and remind you how much exists outside your established model.

5. Physiological grounding

Protect sleep, eat regularly, stay hydrated, move daily. Fatigue, chronic stress, and substances can distort how convincing, threatening, or meaningful your thoughts feel.

6. Cognitive grounding

Ask what you know, what you assume, and what evidence could change your mind. Separate things that happened from the stories you constructed about them.

Here’s the invariant I’d extract

A model of reality stays accurate through contact with information it doesn’t control.

Think about our conversation about roads.

You can construct an elaborate theory of traffic patterns, social organization, and hidden causes.

But eventually, you need to go observe the roads.

Maybe your theory holds. Maybe a school schedule explains everything. Maybe something unexpected changes your entire understanding.

That feedback is what keeps the model grounded.

And the same applies to your personal life.

You can spend hours thinking about being a better father, becoming successful, or achieving happiness.

But playing with your kids, making progress on your business, exercising, and enjoying a beautiful afternoon provide experiences that thinking alone cannot replace.

I’d build a simple daily reality-reset ritual

Daily grounding checklist

0/5

Get outside for 15–20 minutes

Walk without scrolling. Notice sounds, temperature, movement, and your surroundings.

Do one tangible thing

Finish a task with a visible result: repair, clean, build, train, or create.

Connect with someone

Give another person undivided attention, without trying to optimize the interaction.

Reality-check one story

Identify one assumption and ask what actual observations support or contradict it.

Protect your sleep

Keep a reasonably consistent bedtime and make room for recovery.

