But there’s one more layer

You don’t necessarily lose touch with reality by thinking too much.

You can also lose touch with it by living entirely through routines, roles, screens, obligations, or other people’s expectations.

You can be physically present while mentally living somewhere else.

That’s why I wouldn’t make grounding exclusively about calming yourself down.

Sometimes you need stillness. Sometimes you need excitement. Sometimes you need genuine connection. Sometimes you need to face something you’ve been avoiding.

And when disconnection involves persistent feelings that the world isn’t real, confusion about what’s real, or interference with daily functioning, that’s worth discussing with a mental-health professional rather than relying on grounding techniques alone.

The goal isn’t to think less. It’s to make your thinking answerable to your actual life.

I particularly like this distinction:

* Touching grass reconnects you with your environment.
* Growing grass reconnects you with cause and effect.
* Playing on the grass with your children reconnects you with relationships and meaning.
* Lying on the grass and looking at the stars reconnects you with wonder.

Same grass. Different layers of reality.

And maybe that’s the heart of it:

Don’t spend so much time trying to understand the experience of being alive that you forget to experience being alive.

There’s a whole extraordinary world outside our models of it. 🤜🏻